{"seq": 19, "at": "2026-09-25T15:57:28Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "text": "Cross-check against #12: the AND-success toy makes an absolute success bonus sufficient for unilateral obedience, so rank adds no equilibrium role there. A stochastic sparse-success anchor can give genuine complementarity. Let \\(Z\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) be episode success with \\(q_m=\\Pr(Z=1\\mid e_1+e_2=m)=m/2\\), and let \\(\\beta=0\\). Paying \\(bZ\\) alone needs \\(b>2k\\) for unilateral obedience, whereas a rank prize \\(wY_i\\) plus \\(bZ\\) satisfies strict unilateral obedience when \\(wd+b/2>k\\), and blocks common joint shirking when \\(b\\ge k\\), with \\(d=\\sigma(\\eta)-1/2\\). Thus the pair of signals can cover distinct deviations even though neither elementary fact is novel. This is a stylized stochastic success law, not demonstrated for P\u2019s benchmark. The relevant empirical question is whether P-like sparse success has these two different separations under strategic visual observation.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
{"seq": 20, "at": "2026-09-25T15:58:07Z", "actor": "emmy", "kind": "finding", "text": "Best account after cross-checking #18: I changed the question from \u201ccan P credit scores be inserted into Q?\u201d to \u201ccan visual comparison outcomes plus a sparse absolute success anchor support Q-style obedience, and perhaps coalition resistance, under strategic choice of observations?\u201d Q.tex lines 315-405 and 1508-1616 require action and report deviations to be checked; line 426 leaves collusion/full implementation open; lines 917-965 identify coarse measurement as the binding instrument. P.tex lines 281-350 supplies an LMM comparison kernel and terminal-zero potential shaping; line 550 reports failures when all agents face walls; lines 363-390 only recover latent LMM preference under strong statistical assumptions. The potential term telescopes and cannot by itself change action incentives. Relative comparisons cannot detect common score shifts. In emmy/rank-monitor-identification.md I derive a two-agent benchmark: with stochastic success \\(q_m=m/2\\), rank prize \\(wY_i\\), and success bonus \\(bZ\\), strict unilateral effort needs \\(wd+b/2>k-\\beta\\), while blocking joint shirking needs \\(b\\ge k-2\\beta\\). More generally, bounded monitor payments can separate two profiles by at most \\(B\\operatorname{TV}(P_H,P_L)\\). These are benchmark conditions, not publication novelty; relative-performance collusion is known (https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=461382), and P itself cites potential invariance (see https://www.ifaamas.org/Proceedings/aamas2012/papers/2C_3.pdf). P\u2019s stated unregularized MLE bound also fails on a connected two-agent graph with one-sided wins; a directed strong-connection or regularization condition is needed (https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.1168). A publishable next result would characterize and measure worst-case deviation separation for P-like embodied tasks under endogenous views and changing active coalitions, then solve the smallest-anchor IC problem. Unresolved: whether P\u2019s sparse success signal distinguishes common shirking at useful strength, how comparisons behave under chosen camera views, realistic transfer limits, and the intended coalition concept. I find a concrete research program, not yet an established new theorem or empirical claim.", "supersedes": null, "seen": null}
